# Carries the GL driver that pc_select_egl.sh detected, from init_dev_config
# (which may set this) to vendor_init (which may set ro.hardware.egl).
#
# The relay exists because ro.hardware.egl has exactly two permitted writers:
#     neverallow { domain -init -vendor_init } exported_default_prop:property_service set;
# so no script of ours can set it directly, in any domain, at build time. But
# init.pc_x86_64.rc is a VENDOR rc, and property sets in it run as vendor_init,
# which is on that list. So the script reports what it found and init decides.
# vendor_public_prop declares the TYPE, it does not grant read.
#
# That distinction cost a boot. define_prop() (te_macros:922) only emits
#     type $1, property_type, vendor_property_type, vendor_public_property_type;
# where "public" means the type name is part of the policy API that vendor
# policy may reference -- NOT that anyone may read the property. Switching from
# vendor_internal_prop to vendor_public_prop therefore changed nothing at
# runtime: 464 denials in one boot, with the values correctly set and invisible.
#
#     W libc: Access denied finding property "vendor.mesa.nouveau.use.zink"
#
# Reading is granted by get_prop() (te_macros:385) and nothing else. Mesa reads
# these in-process, in SurfaceFlinger, bootanim and every app that touches GL,
# so the read has to be granted to `domain` -- narrowing it to a list of GL
# domains would just be a list of every domain that ever draws.
#
# These are two read-only selection hints (which GPU to render on, whether to
# take the zink path). Writing stays restricted to vendor_init and vendor_shell
# below, which is where the values come from.
vendor_public_prop(vendor_pc_gpu_prop)
get_prop(domain, vendor_pc_gpu_prop)
